 {:seon.task/name "the-turn-message-stays-in-the-scrollback"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.context/messages` rebuilds the turn sections every turn as the last user message and drops the earlier ones, so request k+1 is request k minus its last message. DeepSeek makes cache units at the end of each request's input and output, so the newest scrollback turn sits on no unit and hits only through the lazy shared-prefix units, about one turn behind (~5-8k missed tokens a turn, estimated). Correct: each turn's state message stays in the history after that turn's tool messages (read back from the records it sent, `seon.context/turn-text`); the newest is the current state; older ones may show as a diff against the one before. Prove the prefix stays byte-identical when rendered in two processes (the printer's map order). Measure: hit share per turn on the scripted dry run; v1 got >85%."
