 {:seon.task/name "evaluations"
  :seon.task/plan "The model's context is a sequence of real evaluations, rendered from their records by the one printer's :ai face; no hand-built sections (owner, 2026-10-23: \"stick with a system message that we put in via (help) and then a pull and walk render in simple clojure code so there's no magic and we output data for the responses for the agent and it should be cache stable and grow with transcript size mostly except the dynamic portions which should be at the end\"; \"everything is structured as separate evals where we show the forms the agent input (or we generate the help and agents entity walk) and they can clearly see how results come back with timing and individual renders being called so they can see what functions are rendering what data and where the data is so they can update it to update their context generation intuitively\"; \"everything is real and is structured just like the model ran them and recorded and rendered with render functions not manual wiring\"; \"These need to be cachable so we aren't literatlly re-running things\"; \"now can be a named function\"). Root's ruling (decisions.md, 2026-10-23): no live agent runs until the context has a format agents respond well to; each version is scored by agent-loop's judge (about 10 real task contexts, the first move scored blind) and read by Haiku readers (the scratch paper context-reader.md (git history), from one request: {:op :context :task}); keep each change small enough to score often. What exists: seon.context/render builds the system message and the current-state block from hand-built sections (seon.context/current-state, sections, request-text), seon.turn/basis! records a turn's basis, and the context is remade from it. Build, in order, each step scored: (1) the system message is (help)'s text (my.agent/help), stable: markdown, a doc like any wiki page, whose citations seon.ref/parse reads and the context pulls as it pulls any page's (owner, 2026-10-23: \"If help is best a markdown style response and then we parse it for refs that works better I think\"); it says plainly what the views are made of: the walk, the one printer, each value's declared render, and the page's Tailwind (classes in hiccup, regenerated by bin/build), and it cites the docs an agent needs (docs/render.md, docs/references.md for the citation format, the Tailwind setup) at the depth each should be pulled, so they reach every context through the citation mechanism (owner, 2026-10-23: \"Make sure the help text is clear that we are using tailwind and put any refs in there that need ot be pulled in to documentation\"); (2) turn 0 is evaluations the turn loop makes and records as the model's are (origin :context): (help)'s sections it needs, then (my.agent/walk) of the agent's own entity (seon.render/walk: agent, run, task, plan, the plan's citations at their depth, the proofs and how each proof's db is made, from its namespace's fixtures: agent-loop's step E run 11, the model never found them); (3) each later turn appends the model's calls: each evaluation's ;; thought, form, result by the result's own :ai render, and its cost as recorded when it ran (ticks, ms and bytes: seon.turn/a-turn-shows-its-cost-against-its-budget), rendered from that fact, which never changes after its turn, so it is byte-identical every turn and the cache holds (owner's ruling, decisions.md 2026-10-23: cost numbers belong in the context, so models learn how expensive things are and understand warnings and limits); what moves is only in the last evaluation, (now), after the cached prefix, and (now) holds only what changes the agent's next move (owner, 2026-10-23: \"only put really useful shit in (now) and no adding up each eval time isn't useful\"): no sums of the evaluations' costs, no stored totals; a reused result reads exactly like a fresh one (decisions.md 2026-10-23); a recorded evaluation is rendered from its record and never run again, so every earlier turn renders the same bytes and the provider's cache holds (a cost proof: the prefix is byte-identical across turns, and the context grows with the transcript; proofs: an evaluation's cost text is byte-identical at turn n and turn n+1, and what moves (proofs now, turns left) appears only in (now), after the prefix); (3b) byte-stable, verified by the provider (owner, 2026-10-23: \"I want byte stable context gen that we verify is being cached properly by the models\"): a proof renders a run's context at turn n and n+1 and asserts turn n's text is a byte-identical prefix of turn n+1's but for the last evaluation (seon.context-test/the-prefix-stays-byte-identical-across-turns, kept for the new format); each model turn records the tokens of the prefix it shares with the turn before beside the provider's cache-hit tokens (:seon.turn/usage), and a turn whose hit falls short of that prefix by more than the provider's cache block (DeepSeek: 64 tokens) is an open problem fact naming the turn and the gap, never silence; the run's page shows each turn's hit share; one short paid run of a few turns on one task verifies it against the real provider once the format scores well (task a-stable-prefix-is-sent-once); (4) the last evaluation, each turn, is (my.agent/now), the owner's six (2026-10-23, all approved), each present only when it has something to say: am I done (the proofs red now, each failing assertion's expected and actual, what changed since the last turn); what did I break (problems its own change caused: a proof outside its task now red, a contract gap, a stale wiki section; the merge refuses on these); did the world move (main moved, so merge main; another agent changed a var it changed; a task it needs merged); did someone tell me something (new messages); how much room is left (turns left, a warning only near a limit, no tally); what have I already read (so nothing is read twice). Its value is the full data; its :ai render is compact and beautiful, one line a fact, and the agent digs into the value (its handle) for what the render left out (owner: \"I want this compact and beuriful and the agent can dig into the result if they want more detail that maybe the render hid\"). Its pieces: proofs now, what this run has read already (task the-state-names-what-was-read), the one part that breaks the cache; (5) the citations a string holds are shown as citations with what they resolved to, so an agent learns that writing seon.ref's format in any string field brings the cited data in; (6) which render made which part shows as the measured readers find useful, not more (owner: \"mostly helpful data not just a shitload of data\"). Deletes seon.context's section builders (current-state, sections, the request assembly) as each step replaces them; agent-loop's found and ns-map go when the walk's citations replace them (agent-loop: nothing of theirs depends on them)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.render/walk"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-test/turn-zero-is-recorded-evaluations seon.context-test/a-recorded-evaluation-renders-the-same-bytes seon.context-test/now-is-the-last-evaluation seon.context-cost-test/the-context-grows-with-the-transcript seon.turn-test/a-turn-whose-cache-misses-its-prefix-is-a-problem seon.context-test/an-evaluations-cost-text-is-the-same-every-turn seon.context-test/what-moves-is-only-in-now}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-request-is-a-conversation-of-evals"
  :seon.task/plan "Step 1 of docs/render.md (approved, docs/decisions.md 2026-10-24). The request is a render of the agent's record (owner, 2026-10-24): each record's :seon.render/ai renders its part as provider-neutral messages (`seon.context/eval-messages`, `seon.context/message-messages`), `seon.context/request` composes them in tx order, and one provider format function, `seon.model/wire-messages`, makes the wire shape; :seon.config.ai/replay-reasoning says whether reasoning goes back. A function-calling conversation: per model turn its prose (the reply's content, stored on its first call's eval), its reasoning replayed exactly as returned, its eval calls, and each call's response as a tool message read from its eval record; the messages a turn took sit before its reply; a form the user evaluated in the agent is a user message carrying its response; the screen ends it. One response per call (`seon.repl/response`, :seon.eval/response): {:handle :value :out :err :ex :ms :ticks :bytes}, each key only when present, the forms run in order and stopped by the first exception, printed by the one printer under its handle result/e<n> (`seon.repl/printed`), which holds the response; n counts the branch's evals, so a handle is unique across restarts. One eval record per call (:seon.eval/*, was :seon.cluster.eval/*; :seon.eval/by, was :origin). tool_choice auto: a prose-only reply ends the turn and the run waits for the user. No ;; reaches the model (the eval tool, docs/agent.md). Deletes: `seon.repl/wrap-print`'s render path, the per-form items and handles, `seon.context/fold-evals`, `shown-eval`, `shown-evals`, `transcript`, the run/turn message split's 'Nothing ran' message, `seon.render.ai/items`, the eval tool's thinking argument, :lead on evals."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-test/the-request-is-a-conversation-of-evals-and-the-page
                      seon.repl-test/a-call-is-one-response}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-turn-records-what-it-sent"
  :seon.task/plan "Step 3 of docs/render.md (revision 3): record what each turn sent, once at send time, and /agent/{id}/debug as the :seon.render/html view of those records, drawn by root's render proc from its cache (step 2), with no request builder and no provider format on that path (owner via root, 2026-10-24). Three parts, each reported with its numbers.
(A) The record, at send time (the owner's ruling 2026-10-24: :seon.turn/sent only, no text entities). seon.context/request answers, beside its messages, the records they render, in order: [:section eid t] the help page's own section as of t, [:task eid t] the task as of its run's first turn, [:eval id], [:message id], [:history n] (n earlier records the history budget left out; its message is a function of n). seon.turn/act! writes the turn with its basis and these refs as the request goes, committed, and its reply and usage, or its typed error (:seon.turn/error), when the call ends: one turn entity either way. seon.context/sent-parts reads a turn back, each record by its own render. Deletes the remake path (remade, run-text, recorded). Proofs: seon.context-test/a-turn-records-what-it-sent (read back equals sent, after the task and the help page changed), a-turn-records-one-turns-messages (no text in the record); seon.agent-test/a-failed-call-keeps-what-it-sent.
(B) The debug page. /agent/{id}/debug is a view of the agent entity (web view-of `<id>/debug`, as /agent/{id} is), whose page lists the agent's model turns newest first; each turn's row is its :seon.turn/sent refs, each resolved to its record and shown by that record's :seon.render/ai messages through one :seon.render/html render of a sent request (role, content, reasoning folded, each eval call's prose and code, a tool message under its call, the screen evals set apart, tokens and cache hits from the turn's usage, and beside each part the eval, message or text that made it: the owner's 2026-10-24 ruling, \"styled ... show the form and eval result that created it\"). Drawn by the render proc like any page: its walk is recorded (step 2 B'), a new turn touches it (step 2 C), and only the new turn's row renders (units by input). Reuses: render.proc's page path, ui/page's units, the request render's styling from the set-aside jolt/agent-conversation-debug-wip (its hiccup, not its request rebuild). ~70 lines. Proofs: seon.render-test/the-debug-page-shows-what-each-turn-sent (the page's messages for a turn equal the wire body's, as data), seon.render-test/a-new-turn-is-one-new-row (rendered units: one); checked in a browser on this worktree's root with a scripted run.
(C) Cost proof for the page: one new turn costs one turn's messages (renders: the new row only; the walk is the page's, O(turns) until step 4 makes rows their own components, stated in the proof).
Not in this step: per-entity components and the screen as a read of root's page (step 4); recording the provider's own wire bytes (the record is provider-neutral; the wire body is a pure function of it)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.render/a-view-renders-once-per-change-of-its-reads"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-test/a-turn-records-what-it-sent
                      seon.context-test/a-turn-records-one-turns-messages
                      seon.render-test/the-debug-page-shows-what-each-turn-sent
                      seon.render-test/a-new-turn-is-one-new-row
