 {:seon.task/name "the-screen-shows-the-sections-nearest-the-plan"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-10-25: embed the docs and pull in related concepts that we can refine over the session. What exists: every wiki section has a vector on main (docs/vector.md#what-is-embedded, `{:of :seon.section/address}` restricts ```seon.vector/near``` to sections), the plan has a vector, and the task's data shows only the sections the plan cites (```seon.context/task-data``` :sections). A one-line request cites nothing, so its agent starts with no section and no write set (dogfood, the archived paper dogfood-and-docs-2026-10-25.md (git history) gap 3: about ten evals each of hand exploration). The change: the turn's part (the screen, made again each turn, so it follows the plan as the agent rewrites it; the run's part stays fixed for the prompt cache) shows :related, the k sections nearest the plan's current vector that the plan does not cite, each at summary depth through ```seon.docs/view``` (address and first sentence), with the form that reads one whole (`(my.agent/help \"docs/page.md#anchor\")` once `seon.docs/a-section-reads-at-each-level` merges, else `seon.docs/view`). k is a config key read through the one resolver like `:seon.config.render/similar` (a new key: name it, default, unit and what 0 means, and say why `:seon.config.render/similar` cannot serve; that is the owner's gate, approved in principle by the request above). Computed only when the plan's vector changed (the render cache keyed by its reads: an unchanged plan costs nothing). Measure on the measured requests (`seon.fixtures/requests`, scripted provider, no paid calls): which related sections a scripted agent reads, and tokens per turn; then a paid comparison waits for root's go. Decided (2026-10-25, this worktree): the key is :seon.config.render/related, unit sections, default 5, 0 shows none; :seon.config.render/similar cannot serve: it counts program functions, in the task's data (the run's fixed part, for the prompt cache), and is off by step 6's measurement, while these are sections on the screen that follow the plan. ```seon.context/related``` ranks the sections and ```seon.context/related-at``` reuses its answer while the count and the branch's last vector write are the same. The owner, 2026-10-25, on the first version (an entry of my.task/status): \"I want everything to be explicit evals in our dynamic eval so the embedding references can be dynamic and refined as the session goes on\", and enough shown that agents dig for more: so it is ```my.task/related```, a form of ```seon.screen/forms``` (recorded as an eval each turn), and ```my.agent/related``` for the agent's own text or a var and a count (the screen form asks no effect, so no test reaching the screen reaches a process), each line at summary depth with the forms that read one whole and search further (```seon.context/related-view```); the read form is seon.docs/view until my.agent/help reads any address."
