 {:seon.task/name "the-pages-related-part-reads-what-the-branch-changed"
  :seon.task/plan "```seon.context/relevance``` takes the context's touched items (the vars the branch changed; their mean vector is its zt feature and they seed its PageRank), and on 57 merged tasks' plans with three changed items touched it found 0.481 of the rest of the functions against 0.427 cold, 0.837 of the sections against 0.798 (docs/vector.md#relevance). ```seon.context/related``` takes them as :touched, but neither caller passes them: the page's related part (```seon.render.ui/related```) would have to diff the branch against its base in the render, which reads the whole diff and so runs again (about 100 ms on Seon's 5k texts) at every write the branch makes, and ```my.task/related``` must show what the page shows. The turn already takes that diff once (```seon.agent/branch-changes```, its readers the contract gaps, the merge's divergence and the test plan). The change: the vars the turn's diff changed are a key of ctx (written once a turn by the turn's observe, as its other turn facts), the related component and my.task/related read that key, so both are warm and the component runs again only when the changed set moves. Measure: the component's runs over a ten-turn session before and after, and the related lines a merged task's agent reads (a run's evals naming one)."
