 {:seon.task/name "a-read-draws-one-page-when-it-holds-the-one-before"
  :seon.task/plan "A later read of an agent (```seon.turn/context-block```) told what changed since the read before by drawing that read's page again beside its own (```seon.context/world```: a page at c0 and a page at c1, diffed): on a worktree root of 903 tasks, a later read cost 94M ticks, 317 MB and 25,000 contracted calls, two pages of about 35M ticks and 109 MB each, against an estimated need of 3-6M ticks (work-vs-need #6). ```seon.context/world``` takes c0's page from `pages` ({commit page}, the page the read before drew, which ```seon.context/printed-run``` keeps as :pages and ```seon.turn/context-block``` hands on in the agent's state :pages for every read, an observer's too, which records a turn): 59M ticks, 208 MB and 15,000 calls, each later read the same as the one before it (58.5-59.6M, five in a row). Measured by piece on the same read: the page at c1 35.5M ticks / 109 MB (seven components: the task's data, five cited docs views (```seon.docs/view```) and the related view), observe 6.0M / 36 MB of which ```seon.problems/caused-split``` 4.6M / 28 MB (one :aevt range an attribute), the ctx build and advance 2.6M / 4 MB, printing 2.0M / 9 MB, the rest the request's own. What remains is the page at c1 drawn whole each read: the walk the agent's process already holds live (```seon.repl/render-ctx-hiccup```, ```seon.ctx/ctx``` reacting to each write) redraws only the components whose inputs moved, so the page at c1 is its current hiccup, not a fresh draw (```seon.repl/page-hiccup```, handed to world in :pages), and the cost of a read is the changed components'. Proofs: seon.context-test/a-read-draws-one-page-when-it-holds-the-one-before (the page draws: 2 without the earlier page, 1 with it, the same text told) and seon.server-test/a-later-read-draws-no-page-cold-and-costs-what-the-one-before-did (in a real agent: five reads, none drawing a page cold, each costing within 5% of the one before, the live page the cold draw's at its commit)."
  :seon.task/kind :whole
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-test/a-read-draws-one-page-when-it-holds-the-one-before seon.server-test/a-later-read-draws-no-page-cold-and-costs-what-the-one-before-did}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-measured-requests-name-their-cause"
  :seon.task/plan "Left of audit 2 (main 735daa3660; its table and findings are now held by this task and by seon.context/a-request-is-finished-from-its-page-in-about-twenty-turns, which gates the paid run). Done since, checked in src: a cited var's source and tests are separate children under their own budgets (`seon.render.ui/cited`), tests by reach (`my.agent/tests-reaching`), the related part through `seon.context/related-view` (`seon.render.ui/related`), the plan drawn with its whole form rather than a title copy (`seon.render.ui/prose` :whole), the help request's wording, the `@ctx` paragraph gone from the instructions. Open: (1) F4: the plans of `seon.fixtures/requests` for a-first-sentence-ends-at-its-question-mark and a-name-ending-in-a-star-is-not-cited-as-another do not name the cause the way the print requests do (`Its cause: seon.ref/trim-word strips a trailing *`, `seon.ref/first-sentence`/`lead-sentence`), and those functions are private, so the page did not cite them: audit 2 cost 3-6 turns on the star request. Add the cause sentence to both plans (each reproduction must still answer wrong on main first). (2) F8: `{:to-prove {}}` (about 110 chars, `seon.context/turn-state`'s :to-prove at context.clj:324 is always associated) and a bare empty part reach every first-turn page; the producers return nil for an empty value. (3) The system prompt says the page holds the proofs, the done rule's reasons and the run; say `when it has any`. Proof to write: `seon.context-test`: a first-turn page of a task with no proofs holds no :to-prove key; `seon.template-test/the-measured-requests-are-a-users-with-their-reproduction` keeps the reproductions true."
  :seon.task/kind :work
