 {:seon.task/name "the-agent-page-shows-what-the-agent-knows"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner's design for what an agent is sent and what the person sees (2026-10-28, worked out in conversation; the mock is https://claude.ai/artifact/MRzLTTwTTiTJMdtryzT9pG, which drifted and must be rebuilt from this entry, one example task carried through every part). Agreed so far:

1. ONE CONVERSATION, VERBATIM. The model reads its own past turns exactly as it wrote them (seon.model/wire-message already replays each call as sent); results come back only as tool results, never in the assistant role, never beside the code elsewhere. A cheap check: every assistant message in a request is byte-identical to what the model emitted. Compaction is deferred: get one continuous conversation of prose and forms working first.

2. THE EVAL CALL. Keep today's tool (seon.model/eval-tool): `prose` (markdown, the step's heading, written first) then `code`; a `;;` comment directly above each form is that form's description (seon.repl eval-code already reads it as :lead). Each top-level form is its own eval with its own handle, run in order, the first error stopping the rest. Live test on deepseek-flash (100 runs): verbatim history keeps the format (17/20 and 15/20 vs 8/20 when history was flattened to form->value lines); no answer shape made the model write results into its code (0/100); results-only answers were more reliable than ones echoing the code (20/20 calls vs 16/20); a `;;` line on every form ~70%; no model reused a result handle.

3. THE ANSWER. One result per form, results only (no code echoed), with its handle (result/eN), stopping at an error. OPEN, the owner's objection: the model must be able to tell which handle belongs to which form. Options to decide: the answer carries each form's `;;` description (the model's own words) with its handle; the model names each form; or another. Today the answer is only the last form's value: one answer per form is a change.

4. WHAT THE MODEL IS SENT, in order. System message: text only (instructions, the tutorial convention, the tool). First user message: the task and its context as text and EDN (plan, proofs, cited functions, their tests, users and uses, wiki sections, kept items) plus any setup forms' results as data. Then the turns. A stable prefix that only appends, so a normal turn busts no cache (today's a4 run: ~50% cached, because the request rewrites its front: a history note and older evals collapsing in place). The hiccup sent is semantic (no Tailwind classes or click handlers, which today's is full of). Every eval answer's line carries main's difference from the branch (merges since base, names both changed, would-conflict), computed, never stored.

5. STRUCTURE FIRST. The context is derived every turn from the task's plan: its cited functions (docstring, contract, source one click away), for each the functions it uses and the functions that use it (labelled \"Uses\" and \"Used by\", one-line docstrings, counts), the tests that reach them with status and first failure (proofs first, marked \"done when green\", passing ones collapsed), the citing wiki sections (heading and lead), main's difference. A thin plan shows that; the agent improves its context by citing things in the plan. Ranking only fills a small second tier, later, judged by turns to green (the owner's bar: ~90% relevant, used, fewer turns); tools are taught in the fixed system prompt, never ranked. Append-only: nothing shown leaves.

6. THE SIDEBAR AND KEPT ITEMS. `:seon.agent/sidebar`, a set of refs on the agent's entity, changed by the agent through its ctx atom (`(swap! ctx update :seon.agent/sidebar conj ref)`, a transaction on its branch). Model order: entry order (the asserting t, derived, as seon.context/first-t does); the person's order: pinned first, then last changed (presentation only; the model gets one line saying how the person's view is sorted, changed only when pins change, so no reorder diffs). Each box names the render that drew it (\"drawn by seon.render.ui/test-result\"; an agent's own: `:seon.render/with my.viz/x` on its data). Where a box's content is already in the transcript, the model's copy is the handle (\"(doc x) -> see result/e5\"); the person sees it in full. A task can carry setup forms run as the first evals; a form can keep its result.

7. PHASES. plan -> work -> finish -> done, each a schema over the task (a \"complete plan\" schema: citations resolve, a proof exists and is red, the plan says what it replaces); the instructions are rendered from m/explain of the current phase's schema, so they are derived; passed checks collapse (accretion, nothing swapped). Compaction, later: retract the earlier evals and turns from the agent's branch (history keeps them), end the process, fork a fresh agent on the same branch; process memory is scratch, so what matters is stored as facts first.

8. THE PERSON'S PAGE. User-facing: the conversation only (the person's messages; per call its prose as a heading, then per form its `;;` line and its outcome, derived from the value; a disclosure arrow loads the form and the value on demand, and opening it is a fact (:seon.eval/opened) the agent sees next turn). The input box at the bottom, growing as the person types. The task lives in the sidebar, not the transcript; what the last turn changed is highlighted, computed from the turn's EDN diff (never labels written into the model's copy). The done line is seon.reconcile/done-explain's answer: one line and a handle.

9. THE DEBUG PAGE. The request exactly as sent, in provider roles, as syntax-highlighted EDN; the person-only metadata (time, tokens, cached vs new, which render drew each part, when each entered) in a separate margin labelled as not sent; the cache point per turn, with anything that busts it labelled why; each turn's diff as the EDN it is.

OPEN beyond 3: the per-eval description when a form has no `;;` line (show its code); teaching handle reuse (one example in the instructions); text as data for reading files (`lines`-style readers, args last for ->>), decided from real runs.

Next: one example task from scratch, carried through 1-9; an independent reader with none of this history checks it for gaps; then the mock is rebuilt from it; then the build plan."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-context-is-the-agents-evals-and-seons"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner's design for what the model is sent, agreed 2026-10-29 in conversation (replaces seon.context/world, the page items and their caches, the unsent current-state block, the fold and :same-as, included-tx in the turn, and observe's per-turn work).

The request is the system prompt, then every eval record of the agent in order, each printed by the one printer as a pure function of the record (no fold, no record depending on the ones before), so a request is the one before plus what was added and a cache of a record's text is keyed by its id and evictable (AGENTS 5). A model's eval is its assistant tool call and tool result; an eval Seon makes is the same record, its response naming who asked (:by, the agent's id or root's), sent as a user message since a provider's assistant role holds only what the model wrote.

Turn 0 (the agent's start, no records yet): Seon evaluates and records (my.agent/help), any setup forms, and the render of @ctx (the agent's ui, expanded hiccup, the same the person sees: the one large upfront cost).

Each turn after the model's reply: each eval call runs in order, its result its tool message; then Seon evaluates (my.agent/prove) (the proofs' results land as facts, seon.proofs/test-results-are-a-few-facts) and one changes form answering {:ctx :ui :main}, each left out when empty:
- :ctx, seon.ctx/diff of the agent's record between the last turn's commit and now ({path [old new]}, costs what changed);
- :ui, each render part whose walk revision moved since the last turn's frame ({part-id hiccup}); a list is a part per row (Phoenix LiveView's streams), and a large part that changes often is split, never patched inside;
- :main, how main differs: main's merges since the merge base (seon.merge/divergence of main's side), the names both sides changed, and a would-be conflict (THE PLAN step 1, problem 4).
A reply with prose and no call is the agent winding down: it is kept as the assistant message (today it is lost from the model's history: seon.context/records reads only evals and messages), then Seon runs the proofs and the done rule, records the end and the agent goes idle; green ends it, red tells it why.


Settled since (2026-10-29): the changes eval's value is the diff itself, printed by the one printer at its budget with the forms that read the rest; members that joined the agent's own reverse joins (turns, evals, messages) since the last turn are told by their handle or id, never whole; telemetry and derived data are off the record (seon.ctx/the-record-is-facts-about-the-work), so a turn's diff is its work. Done is every proof green, the merge check's among them (seon.agent/the-agent-is-the-unit-not-the-run cut 2): a conflict, a red test or a missing contract is red; behind is no red (the agent merges main in, as seon.turn/work! does at its start and at each green); the merge check's result is keyed by the branch commit and main's commit; the proofs at done are the merge set, the process tests the change reaches included.

Build (the owner, 2026-10-29: \"put together a plan and lets start working\"), each step its own merge, bin/prove green and reviewed, measured before and after on one session:
R1. The model reads the walk's parts. A request is the system prompt, then the parts of the agent's render of @ctx: at the first request every part whole, in the layout's order; after it, each new part appended, then each part whose walk revision moved since the request before, appended as its new rendering; a removed part draws the request whole (compaction). An eval part is its provider messages (seon.context/eval-messages: the call as the model sent it, its result); every other part one user message of its printed hiccup; a turn's prose reply with no call is its assistant message. The parts each request sent, [part-id revision] with the commit, are the model call's (seon.ctx/the-record-is-facts-about-the-work), so a replay reprints them from the branch at that commit. Deletes seon.context/world, page, page-lists, turn-pages, the page items of request and printed-run, seon.repl/page-hiccup and the agent state's :pages. Proofs: seon.context-test/a-first-request-is-the-help-and-every-part, seon.context-test/a-turn-appends-its-new-and-moved-parts, seon.context-test/a-part-is-sent-once-a-revision, seon.agent-test/a-prose-reply-stays-in-the-history; cost proofs seon.context-cost-test/a-turns-request-costs-its-parts-flat-in-the-session (100 and 1,000 evals) and seon.render-cost-test/a-commit-of-telemetry-redraws-no-part.
R1 as built (2026-10-29, jolt/the-model-reads-the-walks-parts): a request holds the parts that moved as one user message, the model call records [:parts agent commit whole? ids call] per message and last [:hashes {id hash}] (a part's hash is its printed form's, hasch/uuid; the walk's revision is per process, so not that), and the next request compares the live parts with the previous call's hashes; a process with no print of its run reads the previous call's refs back (seon.context/parts-of) and appends; a record the previous call sent that is gone draws the request whole. The remaining proofs are seon.context-test/a-part-is-sent-once-a-change (for a-part-is-sent-once-a-revision) and seon.context-test/a-trimmed-conversation-draws-the-request-whole; seon.render-cost-test/a-commit-of-telemetry-redraws-no-part is not built here.
R2. Root's work after a turn is effects whose facts the parts render: main merged into the branch when it moved (a conflict an unmerged entry), then the proofs the change reaches (the test service, results by digest), then the merge check of the commit (conflicts, contracts), each result a fact on the task keyed by the commit and main's commit (seon.proofs/test-results-are-a-few-facts; the merge-check result of seon.agent/the-agent-is-the-unit-not-the-run's cut 2 step B); the proofs part shows done or the reasons. Deletes seon.turn/observe's per-turn work, seon.context/current-state, turn-state, turn-input and render's :block (refused, callees and failure stay: their input becomes the one value; R2b-1 below). :seon.agent/included is a separate question. Proofs: seon.agent-test/a-turn-ends-with-main-merged-and-its-proofs-as-facts, seon.agent-test/done-is-the-merge-checks-result.
R2b ruled (the owner, 2026-10-29, choosing between options): the agent does its after-turn work itself, in its own process on its own branch, each result a fact there; root only reads them (\"The agent, as facts\"); and R2b is two merges (\"Two merges\"). R2b-1, no behaviour change: the proofs part and the done reasons are drawn from facts at the db's t: each proof's :seon.test/status and :seon.test/failure on its var entity (no fact: unknown), the contract gaps and the problems caused over the branch against :seon.agent/base-commit, plan-before from :seon.run/plan, the unmerged entries (seon.merge/unmerged), the cut reply from :seon.turn/truncated, turns left from :seon.agent/max-turns; it deletes the model call basis's :observed (the taken message ids stay: what was read), seon.turn/observed and observed!, seon.context/turn-input, turn-state, current-state and render's :block, and my.task/status reads the same render. Two inputs had no fact (the owner chose, 2026-10-29: \"Error fact; derive deferred\"): a test run's own error (a fork refused) becomes :seon.process/test-error on the agent's process entity, the error value; the deferred tests are derived: a turn's run leaves out every process test, proofs included (seon.agent/run-selection passes seon.proofs/left-out no proofs), and the done rule asks deferred only of proofs, so the done input's deferred is the task's proofs that are process tests of the program at t (seon.program/process-tests over the graph index, the same index the test plan builds; its cost is read once per db move, never per render). Root's gate keeps :seon.run/deferred. Proof: the context of a recorded session is the same bytes before and after. R2b-2, the behaviour: main merged into the branch each turn it moved (a conflict an unmerged entry), and the merge check result a fact (seon.agent/the-agent-is-the-unit-not-the-run cut 2 step B). :seon.agent/included (the related units, held so the request stays stable for the prompt cache) is a separate question, not in R2b.
R2b-1 as planned (2026-10-29, after the owner's \"algorithmic demands ... simplify code and compose it together\"; \"keep everything as data until it's at a print boundary\"; \"store information we need and its presence means something\"):

The demand. Every reader of \"how the agent's work stands\" needs one computation: the branch's diff against its merge base (seon.merge/content-datoms then delta-changes), and from it the contract gaps (seon.merge/uncontracted), the problems caused (seon.problems/caused-split), what the merge carries (divergence) and, with main, readiness; with the proofs' test facts and the unmerged entries, the done rule's reasons (seon.reconcile/done-explain). Today it is computed twice a turn by two caches that never meet: seon.agent/branch-changes (an identity memo on the agent's held :base and db: observe, the test plan, my.branch/changes) and seon.render.ui/branch-views (its own diff from commit-db of :seon.agent/base-commit, run again on every move of main or the branch). Its results are drawn twice (status: the done reasons from observe's copy held in the basis :observed; branch-views: gaps and caused again), and proof status a third time in task-data and cited.

The one value. A function of (db, the agent) answering the work's standing as data: {:proofs [{:test :status :failures [assertion] :source :calls}] (each proof from its :seon.test/status and :seon.test/failures facts; no fact: unknown; :source and :calls only while not green), :reasons (done-explain over those statuses, the gaps, the caused, the unmerged, plan-before from :seon.run/plan, deferred = the task's proofs that are process tests of the program at t), :refused (seon.context/refused over :reasons: each reason as what to do, the form that does it), :gaps, :caused, :attention, :carries (divergence), :merge (readiness, when the task merges itself), :unmerged, :test-run (:seon.process/test-error, while present), :turn {:n :of} (:seon.agent/max-turns and the turn count), :your-last-reply (from :seon.turn/truncated)}. One diff a (base commit, db t): the agent's branch-changes keyed by commit and t, not object identity, so its render walk and its turn share it; root's cold draw makes it once. Its readers: the done part (replacing status and branch-views in #p-task), the turn's done check (seon.turn/work!'s end and hand-back-end), my.task/status, my.branch/changes.

Data to the boundary. :seon.test/failures replaces :seon.test/failure (owner: \"failures plural and delete failure\"; wipe the standing store when the schema needs it): each failing assertion the map the test child sent ({:type :message :expected :actual :got}; the child's pipe is the print boundary), every one; seon.proofs/result-tx stops printing the first one to a string. A status fact means the verdict at the test's current reach digest: the run's plan knows every test's digest (test-plan's :digests), and result-tx also retracts the status (and its failures) of each test whose recorded :seon.test/digest is no longer its digest and that the run did not run or reuse; else a stray red that reached a changed callee (not selected at a turn: only proofs and changed tests are) would stay red after its digest moved, which the done rule, walking every test with a status, would refuse on and the run's own results never would. Absent: unknown. A test run's own error is :seon.process/test-error on the agent's process, the error value as data (type, message, its data keys; never a Throwable or its frames), present only while the newest run failed as a whole. refused, failure, callees and not-written stay (their input is the one value, not :observed): they build data, and the one printer prints it at the request and the page.

Inbox sends become facts. take! writes each send it takes as a :seon.message fact on the branch (:seon.message/agent, from, body), so its presence means delivered; R1's records read only message facts, so today a send (root's \"main moved\", another agent's) never reaches the model: a regression this fixes.

Deleted. The basis's :observed (the basis is the commit, task, run, turn, max-turns, origin, agent); seon.turn/observed, observed!, input; seon.context/turn-input, turn-state, current-state, taken-messages, render's :block and request's unread :block opt; seon.render.ui/status and branch-views' own diff (its views read the one value). observe shrinks to running the proofs (their facts written by record-results!).

Proofs. The done part prints, for a fixture branch whose facts equal an old :observed, what status and branch-views printed (captured before deleting); drawn from the branch with no agent process it is the same; one diff per (base commit, t): a cost proof that a turn's request and its page's draw together diff once (contracted calls of seon.merge/content-datoms), ticks and bytes before and after on one session; a send taken is a message in the next request; a refused fork writes :seon.process/test-error and a green run retracts it; a red proof's every failure (to the max) reaches the request as data.

Wiki. docs/agent.md#what-you-read, docs/ctx.md (the record), docs/turn.md (what a turn reads), docs/render.md#the-branchs-views.

Order. (1) failures as data + test-error fact; (2) the one value and its cache key; (3) the done part replaces status and branch-views, page checked in the browser with the owner watching; (4) the turn and my.task read it, :observed deleted; (5) sends as facts; (6) review, prove --merge, measure, merge.
R2b-1 as built (2026-10-29, jolt/r2b-after-turn-facts): seon.context/status (the value is named status, the owner's choice over standing), read once a turn from the agent's walk by the page, the model (its model render, status-model), the turn's done check (seon.turn/status-now; a walk that drew none is :seon.turn/no-status, the run :error) and my.task/status; it reads facts about the work only, never the conversation, and while :seon.process/test-error stands no verdict counts. A deleted main test's verdict is recorded on its name; a deferred proof is :seon.test/status :deferred; the run's plan and cap are facts of its run (begin-run!, work!). The turn's test plan still takes its own diff (seon.agent/branch-changes): a turn reads the branch's diff twice (test plan and status), not once. Measured: a status 5.4M ticks / 20 MB on demo/square's branch, 3.0M of it seon.merge/content-datoms' per-attribute descents (the merge owner's); demo/square green in 7 turns from a clean store, 12 before R2b-1.
Parts answer data, two renders beside each other (the owner, 2026-10-29: \"I want the hiccup render to make the data we need to render the web ui and I similarly want the ai render to make the data we send to the model ... different models could have different renders ... make sure we are not doing shit multiple times and we have a clear path to achieving both\"). Today the model is sent the hiccup itself (seon.context/parts-message prints {id hiccup}, component Vars and tags with it) and each request pr-strs and hashes every part to find what changed: the model's input is a by-product of the page's. Correct, on the walk as it is (seon.render/render!: a component in its reaction, its db reads tracked, its output cached by key with a revision): a component may answer a value whose schema it names instead of hiccup (a part); the walk caches it as now, and two renders are declared on that schema (docs/render.md: a render is declared on the value's schema): :seon.render/html, the hiccup the page draws, and :seon.render/model, what the model is sent, given the model in use in its opts (task, model, provider, as config resolves them), so a render may differ by model; its default the one printer's text at the part's budget; seon.model/wire-messages stays the one maker of a provider's wire shape. The model render runs only when a request is made, cached by the part's key and revision (the page never pays for it); what changed since the last request is the revision, so the request's pr-str and hash of every part goes (R1's follow-up). A component that answers hiccup keeps today's path until it moves; the done part (R2b-1) moves first, then the others one at a time.
The page and the context after R2b-1 (2026-10-29 evening, branches jolt/page-follow then jolt/ref-depth on it; not yet proved or merged). Built: (1) every part of #p-task answers data (task, cited, related, status, run, problems: seon.render/part), so the model is sent data and no markup (the owner, on the request's hiccup: \"I am so confused. Turn 1 is the UI being rendered?\"); the task's model render drops what only the page draws. (2) The chat reads as a chat (\"Does that look like a friendly chat window that my mom could use?\"): what was asked first (chat-ask), a turn one message with its steps folded (one click opens every step's code and result), a failure the only mark, a footer with its turn and context tokens, start and end cards from the run and its end facts (the loop's eval not drawn), the head and the status in a person's words, the side panel sticky beside the chat (three columns always). (3) Counts are tokens, never characters: the cut says ~N tokens and seon.print/tokens-text is the one wording (\"build this into the value printer\"); the page's values compact (one-line cells, floats at three decimals on the page only). (4) Sent to model shows each turn's whole request, every message a card with its role, source and tokens, what it shares with the turn before grouped, and the provider's cache point (\"I want to understand what the agent was sent cleanly ... I don't want anything hidden\"; \"Also the cache points\"); tabs named chat, record, sent to model. (5) What an agent writes is Markdown shown to the user: the prompt says so (\"be expressive\"), the page renders eval prose as Markdown, the model reads it as written. (6) The system prompt says how work is done here (namespace, contracted defn, deftest proof, my.agent/prove, done) and the template's premise step applies to a fix only: demo/square, from a clean store, 12 turns before R2b-1, 7 after it, 3 with the recipe, 2 with parts as data (one run each). (7) Related never includes the system pr