References at their depth (the owner, 2026-10-29: \"The old reference system needs to be adapted for the context rendering system ... different levels of detail for different references (functions have args right?)\"; \"think critically about the references and make sure it's helpful and not noise. We control what we are showing and we can still just do previews and the agent can dig in for full details\"): one cited part per citation of the plan (seon.context/citations: first-mention order, one per name at its deepest depth, the task's proofs left out), its view the reference system's own (seon.docs/cited: resolve-ref for a function, summary its arglists and first sentence, doc its contract and doc, code its source; a section, file, task or namespace by its view; a schema key by seon.docs/schema-view, new), a function at doc or code with its direct tests and callers. WIP on jolt/ref-depth (2f9681ec49): load-checked, not built (:seon.render/kind gained :schema). Owed before it merges: previews by default (a part shows what fits a preview and names the form that reads the rest; the agent digs in), measured tokens per citation kind on a real plan, a check that nothing shown is noise (no part the agent never reads), and the prompt's line on what a delimiter pulls.
The md-scan bench, 2026-10-29 (branch jolt/bench-md-scan, deepseek-flash, a clean store, one run each; every run hit the 40-turn cap with its proofs red): run 2 (2.18M tokens) re-sent one cited-code form 22 times for a namespace's requires and read a made-up (ns seon.ref) each time (fixed at its cause: seon.docs/namespace-source; a repeat mark was built and deleted again, by the owner's ruling that a record's text depends only on that record); its first request sent each proof's source and failures twice (fixed: the status alone holds them); run 3 (1.24M) spent 44 evals looking for files (an agent's cwd is an empty directory) and guessing db accessors, then fought an unquoted nat-int? (fixed: the prompt says the program has no files and how a schema entry changes; the refusal names the value and says to quote it); run 4 redefined scan with lists missing :items, and recording its next eval parsed the prose with that scan outside eval-string's boundary, so seon.turn/work! died (fixed: citations are read inside the boundary); run 5 (2.03M) redefined scan at e15 but could not write a 250-line letfn whole: it text-edited a copy in /tmp and spent 20 turns on \"Unmatched delimiter: ]\" (its error named line 224, column 129: the text edits, not the error, were the trouble). Fixed since: a proof's helpers in its own namespace come with their source, identical failures are one entry (seon.context/failures), a cited schema shows the forms of the keys it names and how it changes, never its file. Runs #6-#10 (2026-10-29 night): #6 looped on our part's vague :names key (read as :seon.ref/names; renamed :keys-it-names, and (my.schema/forms k) for an unknown key now names its namespace's keys, never nil); #7 and #8 were ended by Seon's own code meeting the agent's changed program (each turn now binds the branch's schemas; a throw is the agent's, as data, seon.turn/turn-error!); #9 and #10 by the root cause, every draw parsing the plan again with the live seon.ref/scan (a task's cit