Decided after (2026-10-29, the owner: \"This really feels like we are conflating the data with the render\"; \"Don't fear deletion make the system work with the data that's there ... Derive it from the current state\"), superseding the changes form and the ctx diff above: the model's context is the model render of @ctx, declared beside its html render and drawn by the one walk (seon.render/walker) over the same record. Its parts: one per eval in @ctx's :evals (Seon's evals among them: help, prove), in order, each its call and its result; and the other parts (the task, the agent's status, main's merges). A request is the one before, then each new eval part appended, then each other part whose walk revision moved, appended as its new rendering. The agent trims its context with the atom: (swap! ctx update :evals ...) retracts those evals on its branch (history keeps them); a part that left cannot be appended, so the request is drawn whole from @ctx, the one compaction. Main is merged into the branch every turn main moved, before the proofs run; a conflict is an unmerged entry the agent resolves; prove answers {:done true} or {:done false :reasons [...]} as data (each red with its first failure, each conflict, each uncontracted function), with :tests {:ran :reused} and :main {:merged :applied :conflicts}. Nothing derived is held: what the request draws is derived from @ctx at its t.

The model and the person read one render (the owner, 2026-10-29: \"build with both the context render and the web ui render in mind so we don't let one get out of sync with the other\"): today the model reads render-ctx's #p-task parts while the person's main page is chat-ctx, a second layout over the same ctx, so they can drift. One layout serves both: the chat (the conversation) beside the parts the model reads (task, proofs, main, tiles), seon.render.ui/render-ctx and chat-ctx made one; every step after R1 changes the two readers together, and a standing proof holds them equal: seon.render-test/the-model-and-the-page-read-the-same-parts (for an agent at a commit, each part a request sent equals, by id, the hiccup the page's unit of that id renders).
One layout as built (2026-10-29, jolt/r2-one-layout-and-facts): chat-ctx, chat-opening, turn-item and message-item are deleted; render-ctx's #c-rows holds chat-turn (a turn's reply, thinking folded), eval-item, chat-message and chat-end, its #p-task is the parts the model reads, and /agent/<x> is that layout (the agent's own walk while its process runs, root's from the branch otherwise). /agent/<x>/debug is @ctx printed (seon.render.ui/ctx), one unit, not a layout. The proof above is built.
R1 follow-ups (review, 2026-10-29): each request hashes every live part's pr-str, the whole page's work per turn: a part's hash belongs with its walk cache entry, made once a revision as its html is (seon.render/placed-units); seon.context's previous-call lookup queries all of the agent's turns: read the previous model call by its id instead.
Main's moves redraw the branch views (measured by the optimization session, 2026-10-29): render-ctx draws [#'branch-views {} base-commit main-commit], and seon.render/child-key keys a part by its args' hash, so every new main commit is a new element id and a whole redraw (a read ~15.5M ticks / 78 MB when main moved since the read before, ~9.2M / 73 MB when not; main moved in 8 of 13 reads of one shard run). Correct, in R2b where main becomes its own part: a part's id comes from the data it shows (the agent, the task), never a hash of its args; only the part that reads main (what main brought, behind/ahead, a conflict) runs again when main moves; and find what moves main so often while one agent works (root transacting its own facts on most reads would cost every agent's read this in production).
One-layout follow-ups (review, 2026-10-29): /agent/<x>/debug prints the whole unwindowed @ctx as one unit, re-sent on every branch move: a printer budget or a part per key, with a cost proof; seon.render-test/the-model-and-the-page-read-the-same-parts compares the cold draw with the walk by id and order only: compare each unit's html with the model's hiccup by id.
